{"kind":"request","text":"codex: review exact clean pushed head fa9f425046c071d367cc935fa0e9c76d31af06b0. Reporting artifact c549db57 at internal/kernel; it retires the fad32567 one.\n\nYour finding was right and the reason it was wrong is worth naming: writeEventReservingScratch took an io.Writer, so it could not read the output capacity even in principle. It now takes the concrete chunk writer and the preflight subtracts twice the retained capacity along with the rest of the reserve. Your mechanism — gzip buffering, so scratch rises without provoking a write and the writers own check never runs — was the part I had missed rather than mis-stated.\n\nThe test you called non-probative is deleted, not patched. Its replacement drives the production cache, bounded writer, gzip and buffer, grows the real capacity, then raises scratch on an event that provokes no underlying write and asserts that condition rather than assuming it, with every quantity in the limit read out of the production objects. It was proven against fad32567 by restoring that file and adapting only the two call signatures: it fails with scratch one byte past the retained-output budget was admitted.\n\nThe thing I want you to rule on is the fixture, because I would rather you judged it than inherited it. It calls compressed.Flush() after the growth phase, which production does not, because without it gzip tipped the scratch event into an underlying write and the test measured the wrong instant. The flush drains through the production bounded writer so its growth is real, and the no-write condition is asserted afterwards. The alternative is tuning payloads to flates block-emission points, which is more brittle and would rot on a Go upgrade. The test also bypasses appendBorrowedChunk because the defect lives at a mid-build instant that function exposes no way to observe.\n\nThis is the third test in this lane you have had to reject as measuring something adjacent to what it claimed — a slot charge that never forced a growth, a growth budget that shared arithmetic with the constants it checked, and a scratch budget modelled on length where production retains capacity. The rule I am applying now is that a test asserts against values read out of the production objects, never against a formula it computes in parallel. I would rather you told me that rule is still not enough than discover a fourth.\n\nI also checked the three other budget computations in the file without being asked: appends preflight correctly has no output term because both chunk builders run to completion before returning, and the other two carry their own.\n\nGates at this head, run by me: gofmt, git diff --check, vet, GOOS=windows build, and full go test ./... -count=1 all clean, thirty packages, zero FAIL lines.","body":{"branch":"request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound","conditions":"Review exact clean pushed head fa9f425046c071d367cc935fa0e9c76d31af06b0 on request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound, reporting artifact git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c549db57e9595b550ed3aa3d45e56e89a6f6eb16 at internal/kernel, against parent request bc311b36, child requests 80320929 and 1c0efc81, and your ratified verdict 48715fe1. Record all three conclusions. Architecture: whether the kernel contract, compact v3, streamed reads and the 256 MiB serialized ceiling hold. Security: whether the construction peak now holds at every instant including the mid-build instant your verdict named, with the scratch preflight counting the retained output and its growth-copy headroom. Simplification: whether anything can be cut. Rule explicitly on the regression fixture: it calls compressed.Flush() after its growth phase, which the production sequence does not, and it drives the production components directly rather than through appendBorrowedChunk, because the defect lives at an instant that function exposes no way to observe. Say whether that is acceptable or whether you want it built differently. File the verdict with gs review so that it rests on the named artifact. Any change to the head invalidates the verdict.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}